To terminate a C program, you can use the `return` statement with no value or an integer value. The `return` statement immediately terminates the execution of the program and returns control to the operating system.

Here are a few ways to terminate a C program:

1. Using `return` statement with no value:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    return 0;
}
```

2. Using `return` statement with an integer value:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    return 1;
}
```

3. Using the `exit()` function:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    exit(0);
}
```

All of these methods will terminate the program and return control to the operating system. The value returned by the program is typically used by the operating system to determine if the

